course: dictionary
lesson: 12666
title: Dissuade and talking someone out of it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To dissuade someone is to persuade them not to do something. You usually dissuade someone from a plan or idea that is risky or foolish. «Mum tried to dissuade me from climbing the wet tree.» Mum wanted me to stop, not start.
explain: Look for the clues around the word. Dissuade is often followed by «from» and a doing word, such as «dissuade someone from swimming». The «from» tells you what the person was planning to do, and the sentence tells you someone tried to stop them.
explain: Persuade means to get someone to do something. Dissuade works the other way round: it means to get someone to stop, or not start. To check your meaning, swap in «talk out of» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Dissuade is a verb: dissuade, dissuades, dissuaded, dissuading.
fact: Dissuade means to persuade someone not to do something.
fact: Dissuade means to talk someone out of a plan or idea.
fact: A common phrase is «dissuade someone from doing», as in «The sign tried to dissuade people from swimming in the dangerous water».
fact: Safety signs at beaches and lakes often try to dissuade people from swimming where it is not safe.
exercise: «Mum tried to dissuade me from climbing the wet tree.» What does dissuade mean here? | To paint a picture | *To persuade someone not to do something | To run very fast | To help someone do something
exercise: A friend tried to ___ me from jumping into the cold river. Which word fits best? | dazzle | *dissuade | decorate | double
exercise: Which sentence uses dissuade correctly? | We dissuaded our lunch from being hot. | The children dissuaded the sand from their shoes. | *The lifeguard tried to dissuade the children from swimming in the rough sea. | The lifeguard dissuaded the sea from being rough.
test: Grandma tried to dissuade Tom from riding his bike in the dark. What did Grandma try to do? | *Talk Tom out of riding his bike in the dark | Paint Tom's bike bright red | Buy Tom a brand new bike | Race Tom down the hill
test: Which word is closest to the opposite of dissuade, meaning to get someone to do something? | Dissolve | *Persuade | Forget | Forgive
exam: The campers' leader worked hard to dissuade them from crossing the frozen lake, which was cracking. What was the leader trying to do? | Teach the campers to swim | *Talk the campers out of crossing the lake | Help the campers skate across the lake | Take photos of the cracking ice
